# Occupation retrieval gold set — 300 labelled worker utterances.
#
# WHAT THIS MEASURES. The Phase 2 acceptance criterion: with vector retrieval OFF, how
# often do a worker's OWN WORDS reach the right occupation family through L0 (exact) and
# L1 (skeleton) alone? Target: >= 60%.
#
# PROVENANCE — READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING THE NUMBER. These utterances are AUTHORED, not
# mined. `chat_messages` holds zero rows, so there is no production traffic to sample and
# the plan's "from real pseudonymized chat_messages, labelled by ops" is NOT yet
# satisfied. What this set is: realistic Hinglish phrasing written to mirror how workers
# in these trades actually speak, including the misspellings, code-switching and
# particle-heavy constructions the normalizer exists to handle. What it is NOT: evidence
# about the real distribution of utterances. An authored set is biased toward what its
# author thought to write, which is exactly the bias a gold set is supposed to remove.
# Treat the resulting hit rate as an UPPER BOUND and re-derive it from real traffic before
# any launch gate depends on it. Phase 9 owns that re-derivation.
#
# LABELS ARE ISCO UNIT CODES, not occupation ids. NCO is over-granular for conversation:
# "Welder, Gas" vs "Welder, Electric" is a coin flip at occupation level and identical at
# family level, because they share a question pack. Resolving to either is a SUCCESS for
# the interview, so scoring at occupation level would report failure for an outcome the
# product treats as correct. ISCO unit stands in for `profiling_family` until Phase 4
# creates it.
#
# PRIVACY. No worker-derived text. Nothing here came from a transcript, so there is no
# name, phone, employer or place tied to a real person to leak. When this set IS rebuilt
# from real traffic it must go through the pseudonymizer first, and the alias validator's
# digit/@/URL rules are the structural backstop.
#
# UNITS COVERED: 7212 welding · 7531 tailoring · 8153 sewing machine · 8322 light driving
# 8332 heavy driving · 5120 cooking · 7411 electrical · 7112 masonry · 7223 machining
# 5414 security · 9112 cleaning · 9313 construction helper · 7126 plumbing · 7131 painting
# 7115 carpentry · 7233 fitting/mechanic · 5223 retail

